.he "grep (1)                     8/16/26                     grep (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
grep
- search a file for a pattern
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
grep [ option ] ... expression [ file ] ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Grep searches the named files, or the standard input if there are
none, for lines matching a pattern, and copies each line found to the
standard output.  The pattern is a limited regular expression in the
style of ed: . [ ] * ^ and $ have their usual meanings, \( and \)
group, \1 through \9 name a group again, and \< and \> match a word
boundary.
.sp
When there is more than one file, each line found is preceded by the
name of the file it came from.
.sp
Several of those characters are also special to the shell, so it is
safest to put the whole expression in single quotes.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-v	Print the lines that do not match.

.ti -10
-c	Print only a count of the matching lines.

.ti -10
-l	Print only the names of the files that have a matching line, once each.

.ti -10
-n	Precede each line by its line number in the file.

.ti -10
-b	Precede each line by its block number.

.ti -10
-i	Ignore the case of letters, so that upper and lower case match each other.  The spelling -y means the same.

.ti -10
-w	Match the expression only as a whole word, as if it were written \<...\>.

.ti -10
-s	Print nothing; use the exit status to test for a match.

.ti -10
-h	Do not precede a line by the name of its file, even when there is more than one file.

.ti -10
-e expression	Take the expression from the next argument, which helps when it begins with a minus sign.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The -b flag prints the block number, but the port never writes the
counter it reads, so it is always 0.
.sp
A line longer than 512 characters is truncated.
.sp
The exit status is 0 when a match is found, 1 when there is none, and
2 for a bad expression or a file that cannot be read.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
fgrep (1), sed (1), ed (1), sh (1)
